cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use basalt in your work, please cite it as below."
title: "basalt: a hazard checker, assembler and scheduler for NVIDIA consumer Blackwell (sm_120)"
abstract: >-
  On NVIDIA sm_120 there is no hardware interlock on fixed-latency instructions:
  a stall count shorter than the latency of a value the next instruction reads
  produces a wrong answer at full speed, with no fault and no warning. basalt
  measures what the hardware requires, checks whether a schedule's control bits
  meet it, and assigns them from scratch. Each part is validated against the
  vendor compiler's own bytes and against the silicon, by rescheduling every
  kernel in a generated corpus and comparing GPU output byte for byte, and by
  reading 5.2 million instructions of shipped NVIDIA library code held out of
  every table the checker uses.
